{"qid":"Q1154406","names":["Ashikaga Shogunate"],"title":"Ashikaga Shogunate","endonym":{"native":"室町幕府","lang":"ja"},"capsule":"The Ashikaga Shogunate (室町幕府, Muromachi Bakufu) was the second feudal military government of Japan, established when Ashikaga Takauji received the title of Sei-i Taishōgun from Emperor Kōmyō in 1338 after overthrowing Emperor Go-Daigo's Kenmu Restoration. The shogunate reached its zenith under the third shogun Yoshimitsu (1368–1394), who reunified the rival Northern and Southern Imperial Courts in 1392, built the celebrated Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion), and negotiated the Yongle-era tally trade with Ming China. Unlike its Kamakura predecessor, the Ashikaga government depended heavily on the cooperation of regional daimyo rather than direct territorial control, a structural weakness exposed by the Ōnin War (1467–1477), which reduced Kyoto to rubble and inaugurated the century-long Sengoku civil wars. The final shogun Ashikaga Yoshiaki was expelled from Kyoto by Oda Nobunaga in 1573, ending the shogunate in substance if not nominal form.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The shogunate's nominal authority over Japan is well-documented, but mapping its actual territorial control at any given date is complicated by the fluid and often nominal nature of daimyo allegiances; polygon reconstructions are necessarily generalized.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Ashikaga shogunate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashikaga_shogunate"},{"label":"Britannica: Ashikaga shogunate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ashikaga-shogunate"},{"label":"Met Heilbrunn: Muromachi 